Q: Detect dynamically allocated object? A: <p>Very interesting topic. I can see two portable ways in solving the management of the object.</p>
<p><strong>The first one</strong> is to create a class with a single pointer as a member data, and to always manage the class through that pointer. So there will be no difference, whether the class is created dynamically, locally, statically, globally etc. The class could have a copy constructor, which will allow to pass it as an argument. The class data could be declared as internal private structure, then the object creation, management and deletion will be done through that structure. The pointer to that structure could be opaque (of <code>void*</code> type), which is very common technique using object handlers. The derived classes must have then their own mechanism to handle their additional data.</p>
<p><strong>The second solution</strong> is to overload the <code>new</code> and <code>new[]</code> operators within the class, as <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/596781/kerrek-sb">Kerrek SB</a> said. This works perfectly with base and derived classes. The overloaded functions have to set internal thread-safe byte counter of the allocated space. This allows to determine in the class constructor, whether or not the overloaded function was called. If the counter is non-zero, the class constructor subtract the object size and set <code>m_Allocated</code> flag. If the derived class has additional data, its constructor has to call the protected <code>add_bytes()</code> member function, to subtract the additional bytes from the counter. Both <code>new</code> and <code>new[]</code> operators call the same <code>alloc_class()</code> member function. The counter must be zero at every allocation call, so the mismanagement can be easily caught.</p>
<p>Here is working portable example. It could be improved with class allocation mismanagement handling, or bad allocation handling:</p>
<pre><code>
#include &lt;cstdlib&gt;
#include &lt;cstring&gt;

class BaseClass
{
public:
    int m_BaseData[9];
    BaseClass() {
        if (s_AllocBytes &gt; 0)
        {
            s_AllocBytes -= sizeof(BaseClass); // subtract base size
            m_Allocated = true;
        }
        else m_Allocated = false;
        std::memset(&amp;m_BaseData, 0, sizeof(m_BaseData)); // initialize base class data
    }
    bool IsAllocated() const { return m_Allocated; }
    static void* operator new(size_t size) { return alloc_class(size); }
    static void* operator new[](size_t size) { return alloc_class(size); }

protected:
    static void add_bytes(size_t addBytes) {
        if (s_AllocBytes &gt; 0) s_AllocBytes -= addBytes; // subtract additional size
    }

private:
    bool m_Allocated;
    static thread_local size_t s_AllocBytes;
    static void* alloc_class(size_t size) {
        if (s_AllocBytes != 0) { /*... class alloc mismanagement ...*/ }
        s_AllocBytes = size; // set allocated size
        if (size == 0) ++size; // avoids std::malloc(0) to return null
        void* ptr = std::malloc(size);
        if (!ptr) { /*... bad alloc handling ...*/ }
        return ptr;
    }
};
thread_local size_t BaseClass::s_AllocBytes = 0;

class DerivedClass : public BaseClass
{
public:
    int m_DerivedData[20];
    DerivedClass() {
        add_bytes(sizeof(DerivedClass) - sizeof(BaseClass));
        std::memset(&amp;m_DerivedData, 0, sizeof(m_DerivedData)); // initialize derived class data
    }
};

int main()
{
    BaseClass bc1;
    BaseClass bc2[3];
    DerivedClass dc1;
    DerivedClass dc2[3];

    BaseClass* pbc1 = new BaseClass;
    BaseClass* pbc2 = new BaseClass[3];
    DerivedClass* pdc1 = new DerivedClass;
    DerivedClass* pdc2 = new DerivedClass[3];

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
